    <section>
        <h2>Parallelogram law of vector addition</h2>
        <p>
            Given vectors {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{b}\\)`}, their sum {`\\(\\vec{a} + \\vec{b}\\)`} can be obtained geometrically via the following process:
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>Draw {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{b}\\)`} such that their <em>tails</em> meet at some point (say O).</li>
            <li>Complete a <em>parallelogram</em> OACB as shown in the diagram below.</li>
            <li>Draw a vector from O to C (say {`\\(\\vec{c}\\)`}). This vector is the sum {`\\(\\vec{a} + \\vec{b}\\)`}. </li>
        </ol>
        <p>The law that says that the diagonal is the vector sum is known as <em>parallelogram law of vector addition</em>.</p>
