    <section>
        <h2>Magnitude and direction of the sum</h2>
        <p>
            In general, we represent the magnitude of a vector {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} by {`\\(a\\)`}. Then, what is the magnitude and direction of {`\\(\\vec{a} + \\vec{b}\\)`} ? We can use a little geometry and trigonometry to obtain that from the parallelogram law of vector addition.
        </p>
        <p>
            We'll make an assumption that the angle between {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{b}\\)`} is {`\\(\\theta\\)`}. Let the angle between {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{c}\\)`} be {`\\(\\alpha\\)`}.
        </p>
