        </div>
        <p>The magnitude {`\\(c\\)`} is equal to the length of the diagonal OC. Therefore,
            {`
                \\begin{eqnarray}
                    c^2 &=& OC^2\\\\
                        &=& OE^2 + CE^2\\\\
                        &=& (a + b\\cos\\theta)^2 + (b\\sin\\theta)^2\\\\
                        &=& a^2 + 2ab\\cos\\theta + b^2
                \\end{eqnarray}
            `}
        </p>
        <p>
            This implies {`\\[c = \\sqrt{a^2 + 2ab\\cos\\theta + b^2}\\]`}
        </p>
        <p>
            At the same time, we have
            {`
                \\begin{eqnarray}
                    \\tan\\alpha &=& \\frac{CE}{OE}\\\\
                        &=& \\frac{b\\sin\\theta}{a + b\\cos\\theta}
                \\end{eqnarray}
            `}
        </p>
